NJ05 independent numeric-admission supplement — legacy input only
Base product revision: 729bfb4b4073d815c7ad81bc80de4826997e4be2.
This ledger extends legacy-boundary.cases; the six old JSON inputs below are generated in memory by tests, never stored as application output. SHA-256 covers the exact UTF-8 input without BOM or terminal LF. These inputs are not canonical new-wire bytes. Decoder admission and host admission are separate facts.

Use P, V and F from legacy-boundary.cases. Repeat one ASCII byte to reach the stated complete input length; the prefix and suffix are included in that length.

  PG-10000001: P + (`x` * 9,999,896) + `"}`; size 10,000,001; SHA-256 b435649e0b0473b7b84c770fddd9b126ae065af3d8a7bbf3c8553efc763d59c9. Old common ProgressCodec.decode accepts and retains every unknown string unit. The 10,000,000-byte iOS/macOS/Android progress host caps reject this input before decoding or writing; host rejection must be executed separately.
  VO-10000001: V + (`x` * 9,999,920) + `"}`; size 10,000,001; SHA-256 70cbd2abdefcfe97c38e1d8bfd263526cbcc53e227308aa103c02fe76bcbebde. Old common VocabularyCodec.decode accepts but discards the unknown `padding`; the common VocabularySession.importJson byte/length gate rejects before a repository write. This is decoder admission only, not a lossless migration control.
  PF-1000001: F + (SPACE * 999,982) + `}`; size 1,000,001; SHA-256 91f9fd8b5b774d22db0013807113421d8f31a36c0d899e5227a5f5502961ead9. Old common UserPreferencesCodec.decode accepts; iOS/macOS host import byte gates reject separately.

Progress depth-256 recipe: P without final `"` + (`[` * 256) + `0` + (`]` * 256) + `}`. Size 616; SHA-256 9bb0ed284da85266484d825da537c2bf48d743911cd1e7d3fe5947f20baa4b1c. Valid old document retains exactly 256 nested arrays. This is a lower bound, not an upper depth limit.

Progress rows-32768-at-limit recipe: P without final `"` + `[` + comma-join for i = 0..32767 of `{"i":` + shortest decimal i + `}` + `],"padding":"` + (`x` * 9,617,777) + `"}`. Size 10,000,000; SHA-256 1dac4ea86cfb3ecf22ef49695ef335ae1381df21c424030f645d0c36a0d1a1a0. Valid old document retains every ordinal in order and the full padding value. It establishes 32,768 list items, 32,768 nested maps and 32,768 scalar rows at the input ceiling; it does not establish a maximum row count or a safe new-wire byte cap. A target with insufficient memory may be NOT RUN rather than treated as semantic rejection.

Progress UTF-16 recipe: `{"version":1,"cards":[],"stats":{},"reviewsToday":0,"lastDay":"2026-03-29","totalReviews":0,"unknown":{"żółć":"😀\t\n","escaped":"\ud83d\ude00","lone":"\ud800"}}`. The Polish key and first emoji are literal UTF-8; TAB/LF and the second emoji/lone high surrogate use literal backslash escape spellings in the input. Size 167; SHA-256 986067549b5dba3c1828b87c5859634053c1fe23718a668d211f6808b8423c7a. The old decoder should retain the two-unit emoji and one unpaired high-surrogate code unit; any target divergence is a migration blocker, not permission to normalize.

Independent semantic expectations: P source retains unknown tree and insertion order; V decoder projects its six known fields and loses `padding`; F whitespace has no meaning. Exact input ceiling is host policy, not a generic wire physical ceiling. The old parser may accept plus-one inputs that the host refuses. No N26 numeric ceiling is frozen here, because the new codec and accepted legacy envelope across all hosts remain unproved.

Independent A/C size arithmetic, assuming a lossless typed projection of the listed progress object in source key order and generic kind `progress`, schema `2`: the single-string PG-10000000 tree is 10,000,112 A bytes and 40,000,022 C bytes including header/digest/final LF. The rows-32768-at-limit tree is 10,196,726 A bytes and 39,214,145 C bytes, with 65,545 total preorder rows. `check_numeric_sizes.py` counts row framing, key token lengths and UTF-16 unit expansion without calling a candidate encoder or constructing output bytes. These numbers are physical lower-bound controls for A/C; they do not assert that a document adapter exists or set an output cap. The vocabulary `padding` field is currently discarded, so no lossless vocabulary output size is asserted from VO-10000000.
